   "id": "yClcOMFXBgAJ",
   "from": "Azman Sami",
   "author": "2960e137b5c32379",
   "date": "2021-10-08T01:25:55Z",
   "subject": "Interpreting coef for gpcm/nominal",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Phil,<div>I am working with a set of questions that allows 2 attempts per question. I have fitted a gpcm model where attempts are considered as categories. Therefore, answer can be 0, 1 and 2. Where 0 = incorrect answer in two attempts, 1 = answered correctly in 2nd attempt, 2 = answered correctly in 1st attempt. Note, the reason I used &#39;1&#39; for 2nd attempt and &#39;2&#39; for first attempt is that I wanted the answer to be ordered by theta. </div><div>Once I fit the model with &#39;gpcm&#39;, I am getting the following parameters. (refer to the screenshot pls). My expectation was that <b>items.d0 &lt; items.d1 &lt; items.d2 </b>given that I have ordered my categories (likert scale). That is indeed the case for most of the questions. However, for some questions, this expectation is not met,  <b>items.d2 &lt; items.d1</b> (pls refer to the yellow highlighted questions).</div><div><br></div><div>My question is:</div><div>1) How should i interpret the items.d* where <b>items.d2 &lt; items.d1 ?</b></div><div>2) When computing the probability of selecting a category, P(x=k), can you use the absolute value of the items.d instead of considering the negative sign? order of value is preserved if I ignore the negative sign.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/mirt-package/attach/657c1385c29c8/image.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"image.png\" width=\"452\" height=\"368\"><br></div><div><br></div><div>regards,</div><div>Azman Sami</div><div><br></div></div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "gIE6dn9bBgAJ",
   "from": "Hugo Harada",
   "author": "73fb61795976b321",
   "date": "2021-10-08T02:34:30Z",
   "subject": "Re: Interpreting coef for gpcm/nominal",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">Azman,</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">The d parameter is hard to interpret. Use b instead. It is called the difficulty parameter and it should behave as you expected. Not sure it will but I really liked the way you framed the problem. Please report back.</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">a(theta -b) = a*theta -a*b=a*theta + d </div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">argument IRTpars will return your coefficients in b format...</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">coef(mod_mt,simplify=TRUE,<b>IRTpars</b> = TRUE)<br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">Good luck!</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">Hugo</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
