  {
   "id": "FnpdIT7jBgAJ",
   "from": "Azman Sami",
   "author": "2960e137b5c32379",
   "date": "2021-10-09T20:02:03Z",
   "subject": "Re: Interpreting coef for gpcm/nominal",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thank you Hugo and Phil for the pointers. Let me try that out and get back.<div><br></div><div>Regards,</div><div>Azman Sami</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Fri, Oct 8, 2021 at 1:32 PM Phil Chalmers &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">rphilip....@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Azman,<div><br></div><div>To further Hugo&#39;s recommendation, you may find playing around with the mirt::itemplot(shiny=TRUE) interface useful for getting a feel for how/what the supported response models are capable of fitting and how this is reflected in the parameters (and in the classical parameterization transformations for unidimensional models). I generally recommend to those new to the package to get a feel for the IRT models that they are less familiar with first before trying to fit them in practice. HTH.</div><div><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Phil</div></div></div></div><br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Thu, Oct 7, 2021 at 10:34 PM Hugo Harada &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">hugo....@adaptativa.com.br</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">Azman,</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">The d parameter is hard to interpret. Use b instead. It is called the difficulty parameter and it should behave as you expected. Not sure it will but I really liked the way you framed the problem. Please report back.</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">a(theta -b) = a*theta -a*b=a*theta + d </div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">argument IRTpars will return your coefficients in b format...</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">coef(mod_mt,simplify=TRUE,<b>IRTpars</b> = TRUE)<br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">Good luck!</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">Hugo</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">Em qui., 7 de out. de 2021 às 22:25, Azman Sami &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">md.azm...@gmail.com</a>&gt; escreveu:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Phil,<div>I am working with a set of questions that allows 2 attempts per question. I have fitted a gpcm model where attempts are considered as categories. Therefore, answer can be 0, 1 and 2. Where 0 = incorrect answer in two attempts, 1 = answered correctly in 2nd attempt, 2 = answered correctly in 1st attempt. Note, the reason I used &#39;1&#39; for 2nd attempt and &#39;2&#39; for first attempt is that I wanted the answer to be ordered by theta. </div><div>Once I fit the model with &#39;gpcm&#39;, I am getting the following parameters. (refer to the screenshot pls). My expectation was that <b>items.d0 &lt; items.d1 &lt; items.d2 </b>given that I have ordered my categories (likert scale). That is indeed the case for most of the questions. However, for some questions, this expectation is not met,  <b>items.d2 &lt; items.d1</b> (pls refer to the yellow highlighted questions).</div><div><br></div><div>My question is:</div><div>1) How should i interpret the items.d* where <b>items.d2 &lt; items.d1 ?</b></div><div>2) When computing the probability of selecting a category, P(x=k), can you use the absolute value of the items.d instead of considering the negative sign? order of value is preserved if I ignore the negative sign.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/mirt-package/attach/6e33e215d7a16/image.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"image.png\" width=\"452\" height=\"368\"><br></div><div><br></div><div>regards,</div><div>Azman Sami</div><div><br></div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;mirt-package&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mirt-package...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nTo view this discussion on the web visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/mirt-package/CABZmTxHD3zAPAaGX1XWozYo9gpMjJcqmUg9StxPuojHkiH5GMw%40mail.gmail.com?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/mirt-package/CABZmTxHD3zAPAaGX1XWozYo9gpMjJcqmUg9StxPuojHkiH5GMw%2540mail.gmail.com?utm_medium%3Demail%26utm_source%3Dfooter&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792481381920000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3et-ffABh6PLrd2jf8Qey7\">https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/mirt-package/CABZmTxHD3zAPAaGX1XWozYo9gpMjJcqmUg9StxPuojHkiH5GMw%40mail.gmail.com</a>.<br>\r\n</blockquote></div><br clear=\"all\"><div><br></div>-- <br><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div><br><table width=\"100%\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;;font-s